er establishing this  system the legislature and all of its  wisdom  made those bond slips negotiable  instruments or at least to the extent  that a defendant could endorse it  payable directly to an attorney  so that was the birth of the hustler  people would walk into court maybe  they'd been charged with shoplifting or  maybe a traffic offense or whatever and  they'd have a bond slipped in their hand  and what jim tooie observed in 1980 when  he went out and looked at these branch  courts were the judges and court  personnel  steering these defendants to particular  lawyers  now at the time of course we couldn't  prove why they were steering them but we  knew that it wasn't because the judges  had great confidence in the legal  ability of these lawyers  after we ran this story the federal  government picked that up they checked  the bond slip refunds because there were  records in the and they were able to  show that  these attorneys weren't paying federal  income taxes on all the money that they  were receiving  and they were ultimately able to  persuade those attorneys to testify  against judges they were paying off  and that became the major  uh the centerpiece of operation greylord  where's name come from  uh the uh there are different stories  about where the the name came from  uh we were told initially in fact the  u.s attorney  the recent u.s attorney dan webb told me  that  grey lord was a sarcastic reference to  the english lord  chancellors and  that's the english spelling of gray as  you will see  someone said more recently that actually  there was a racehorse named greylord and  that they just pulled a name out of the  out of the air  i don't really know there are different  perceptions  you made some reference in the early  chapters about the fbi being predisposed  towards sting operations at the time we  talk about that yes  sting operations are obviously  they draw a great amount of publicity  they get  heavy coverage on television in the  newspap