n't really happen quite like  that  and so the police then had to be taken  in on the  investigation they had to be assured  that they weren't the targets of  whatever the fbi was doing and they were  and so they established a few more that  they  really did get cases into the system  except it's extremely costly  very hard to do in the first place we  had more than 300 judges in the cook  county circuit court at that time and  they sat in various divisions but the  assignments  the case assignments were random  so if you suspected that  a judge was crooked  even if you staged a case you had no way  of assuring that it would get assigned  to the judge where you wanted to attempt  to make the payoff  the  there are other problems i mean for  instance they had an fbi agent uh  ram a car into a light post and he  poured liquor all over himself and and  had just enough so there would be some  on his breath  the police came it was close to the end  of a shift and they didn't want to go  through the paperwork so they just threw  the guy's keys down the gutter and  poured the booze out and sent him home  so they'd go through all of these  elaborate things and then sometimes it  would all come to naught because it  simply wouldn't work  is that sort of a side story to your  book or do you hope also to show that  these kind of operations by the fbi  aren't the best way to go about it my  personal view is that it really isn't an  efficient way  to  investigate corruption that's not the  main point you're talking about oh no no  i mean uh there was certainly uh  was and i believe continues today to be  very  serious  corruption in the circuit court of of  cook county  and this investigation has certainly  gone a long way  toward making the people who are engaged  in that very uncomfortable if not  rooting it out entirely  your book seems to have a number of  villains  and when i read one review and it said  it reads like a novel one of the things  that strikes you is really how blatant  some of  the the actions were how  some of the quotes are almost outrageous  almost as if they were written by a  hollywood scriptwriter as opposed to  well yes now you understand that of  course these things came out in the  trials and  these weren't things that you or i could  go into a courtroom and observe if you  were looking like  i mean wayne olsen one of the judges who  was convicted saying i like people that  take dough because you know where they  stand  or  judge raymond sodini when he saw court  personnel in the back of his courtroom  who appeared to be bickering over  splitting up payoffs that they'd  received from an attorney he told his  bailiff go tell us guys if they're  talking about money to go in the back  room and do it not to talk about it you  know out here in the open well all of  those kinds of things came  out actual testimony  in trials but they weren't something  that we could have observed as  journalists or any other  person who just comes into the court  could see  now you could see a lot of things that  were going on but jim toohey and i had  been covering the courts in cook county  off and on for  more than 20 years  and if you had  asked me to come testify before a grand  jury i wouldn't have a single incident  that i could ever have related  which is why  it takes a massive federal investigation  of this sort  to root it out who are the victims in  stores like this well of course we the  citizens  are the ultimate victims if the  if the justice system  is corrupted we're victims in a number  of ways obviously  if  the drunk driver isn't fined or  something we're financially hurt but  that's a minor part of it  i think that it you know it breeds  disrespect for the law it  in it creates more  lawlessness  than we would otherwise have  and the cook county circuit court  had the reputation  of being corrupt and has the reputation  today  of being corrupt and i think that's the  real  damage that's done  by these  appearances that justice is not done  or that a case can be fixed some judges  can be wired  and another aspect of this corruption  just because of the reputation of the  system  the average person who was charged with  a misdemeanor believed that the system  was corrupt  so if a lawyer said to him well my fee  will be 500  and then comes back  right before the trial or  usually a bench trial and says  well we're going to have to pay off the  judge it'll be another 250.  it was a way that it just enabled  lawyers to extort money  in that way that was called rain making  by the people who engaged in it  that is telling the client that the  lawyer was going to pay a bribe but  actually of course just pocketing the  money  and that was possible simply because of  the reputation  of the system  interestingly  lawyers could tell i mean of course  because of  the  the practice that  this is the evidence maybe a witness is  not going to show up or the evidence is  very weak  the defendant's going to be acquitted  for sure  the lawyer would know and so you could  do this with a great deal of certainty  but  in a case where the lawyer was surprised  if the judge heard the evidence and said  no i find the defendant guilty the  lawyer would then go back and say  gee who have you upset in the  prosecutor's office there was tremendous  heat in this case and the judge just  couldn't do it here's your money back  in most cases were we talking about  large sums of money  well in some cases we were talking about  oh 500 a thousand dollars but in  most cases in some cases we were talking  about extremely small amounts of money  i think one of the things that  amazed  observers in chicago was not that the  corruption was so rife  but that justice was being sold for so  little  some in some cases as little as ten  dollars a case  early on in your book you tell the story  um struck me of one particular judge who  occasionally didn't want to come to  court oh yes we tell that one well  that's judge sadini who ran a  he presided over a gambling court at a  central police headquarters and early in  the morning he had to handle what's  called the bum call  this is the call of all the people who  were swept off the streets basically for  safe keeping  on any given night in chicago they're  all brought in well  judge sadini who drank a lot  would sometimes  have a hangover in the morning and he'd  call in and tell his bailiff to tell the  police sergeant  assigned to his courtroom to put on the  robes and do the bum call so the police  sergeant would  literally put on the judge's robes sit  there and go through this call and these  defendants were all just dismissed  they were  charged with  an offense known as disorderly conduct  which is  really not much of an offense they're  more disheveled and  and pathetic then disorderly  and they're all released  but one of the stories that we told here  one day  a  drunk was being particularly boisterous  in the back and they  saved his case until last and  brought him in and sentenced him to  death it was a rather  unsettling way for him to begin the day  i would suspect  talked about the victims and certainly  the many villains as it were in the  story  are there any heroes are there any good  guys in this book  well i would say  the best guy  was a chicago lawyer named keith davis  who  when we did our initial expose on  hustling in 1981  was the person who went around to the  courtrooms with us showed us what was  going on sort of  exposed a lot of the conduct that  ultimately became the central piece of  greylord the prosecutors by and large of  course we portray as very politically  ambitious people i think a lot of them  were  well motivated did a very good job  and  were certainly not anti-heroes but i  mean they were just doing their job  to me  keith davis would be the hero of  greylord and you and mr tui were working  where at the time  a chicago lawyer is a  it's a monthly publication that it's now  11 years 