n't really happen quite like  that  and so the police then had to be taken  in on the  investigation they had to be assured  that they weren't the targets of  whatever the fbi was doing and they were  and so they established a few more that  they  really did get cases into the system  except it's extremely costly  very hard to do in the first place we  had more than 300 judges in the cook  county circuit court at that time and  they sat in various divisions but the  assignments  the case assignments were random  so if you suspected that  a judge was crooked  even if you staged a case you had no way  of assuring that it would get assigned  to the judge where you wanted to attempt  to make the payoff  the  there are other problems i mean for  instance they had an fbi agent uh  ram a car into a light post and he  poured liquor all over himself and and  had just enough so there would be some  on his breath  the police came it was close to the end  of a shift and they didn't want to go  through the paperwork so they just threw  the guy's keys down the gutter and  poured the booze out and sent him home  so they'd go through all of these  elaborate things and then sometimes it  would all come to naught because it  simply wouldn't work  is that sort of a side story to your  book or do you hope also to show that  these kind of operations by the fbi  aren't the best way to go about it my  personal view is that it really isn't an  efficient way  to  investigate corruption that's not the  main point you're talking about oh no no  i mean uh there was certainly uh  was and i believe continues today to be  very  serious  corruption in the circuit court of of  cook county  and this investigation has certainly  gone a long way  toward making the people who are engaged  in that very uncomfortable if not  rooting it out entirely  your book seems to have a number of  villains  and when i read one review and it said  it reads like a novel one of the things  that strikes you is really how blatant  some of  the the actions were how  some of the quotes are almost outrageous  almost as if they were written by a  hollywood scriptwriter as opposed to  well yes now you understand that of  course these things came out in the  trials and  these weren't things that you or i could  go into a courtroom and observe if you  were looking like  i mean wayne olsen one of the judges who  was convicted saying i like people that  take dough because you know where they  stand  or  judge raymond sodini when he saw court  personnel in the back of his courtroom  who appeared to be bickering over  splitting up payoffs that they'd  received from an attorney he told his  bailiff go tell us guys if they're  talking about money to go in the back  room and do it not to talk about it you  know out here in the open well all of  those kinds of things came  out actual testimony  in trials but they weren't something  that we could have observed as  journalists or any other  person who just comes into the court  could see  now you could see a lot of things that  were going on but jim toohey and i had  been covering the courts in cook county  off and on for  more than 20 years  and if you had  asked me to come testify before a grand  jury i wouldn't have a single incident  that i could ever have related  which is why  it takes a massive federal investigation  of this sort  to root it out who are the victims in  stores like this well of course we the  citizens  are the ultimate victims if the  if the justice system  is corrupted we're victims in a number  of ways obviously  if  the drunk driver isn't fined or  something we're financially hurt but  that's a minor part of it  i think that it you know it breeds  disrespect for the law it  in it creates more  lawlessness  than we would otherwise have  and the cook county circuit court  had the reputation  of being corrupt and has the reputation  today  of being corrupt and i think that's the  real  damage that's done  by these  appearances that justice is not done  or that a case can be fixed some judges  can be wired  and another aspect of this corruption  just because of the reputation of the  system  the average person who was charged with  a misdemeanor believed that the system  was corrupt  so if a lawyer said to him well my fee  will be 500  and then comes back  right before the trial or  usually a bench trial and says  well we're going to have to pay off the  judge it'll be another 250.  it was a way that it just enabled  lawyers to extort money  in that way that was called rain making  by the people who engaged in it  that is telling the client that the  lawyer was going to pay a bribe but  actually of course just pocketing the  money  and that was possible simply because of  the reputation  of the system  interestingly  lawyers could tell i mean of course  because of  the  the practice that  this is the evidence maybe a witness is  not going to show up or the evidence is  very weak  the defendant's going to be acquitted  for sure  the lawyer would know and so you could  do this with a great deal of certainty  but  in a case where the lawyer was surprised  if the judge heard the evidence and said  no i find the defendant guilty the  lawyer would then go back and say  gee who have you upset in the  prosecutor's office there was tremendous  heat in this case and the judge just  couldn't do it here's your money back  in most cases were we talking about  large sums of money  well in some cases we were talking about  oh 500 a thousand dollars but in  most cases in some cases we were talking  about extremely small amounts of money  i think one of the things that  amazed  observers in chicago was not that the  corruption was so rife  but that justice was being sold for so  little  some in some cases as little as ten  dollars a case  early on in your book you tell the story  um struck me of one particular judge who  occasionally didn't want to come to  court oh yes we tell that one well  that's judge sadini who ran a  he presided over a gambling court at a  central police headquarters and early in  the morning he had to handle what's  called the bum call  this is the call of all the people who  were swept off the streets basically for  safe keeping  on any given night in chicago they're  all brought in well  judge sadini who drank a lot  would sometimes  have a hangover in the morning and he'd  call in and tell his bailiff to tell the  police sergeant  assigned to his courtroom to put on the  robes and do the bum call so the police  sergeant would  literally put on the judge's robes sit  there and go through this call and these  defendants were all just dismissed  they were  charged with  an offense known as disorderly conduct  which is  really not much of an offense they're  more disheveled and  and pathetic then disorderly  and they're all released  but one of the stories that we told here  one day  a  drunk was being particularly boisterous  in the back and they  saved his case until last and  brought him in and sentenced him to  death it was a rather  unsettling way for him to begin the day  i would suspect  talked about the victims and certainly  the many villains as it were in the  story  are there any heroes are there any good  guys in this book  well i would say  the best guy  was a chicago lawyer named keith davis  who  when we did our initial expose on  hustling in 1981  was the person who went around to the  courtrooms with us showed us what was  going on sort of  exposed a lot of the conduct that  ultimately became the central piece of  greylord the prosecutors by and large of  course we portray as very politically  ambitious people i think a lot of them  were  well motivated did a very good job  and  were certainly not anti-heroes but i  mean they were just doing their job  to me  keith davis would be the hero of  greylord and you and mr tui were working  where at the time  a chicago lawyer is a  it's a monthly publication that it's now  11 years old and we've done  investigations into  corruption in the judiciary and the  legal profession for  for these 10 years and that's where we  why we got onto this story so early on  and were able to find out so much about  it how long was it before  the city's major daily newspapers were  writing about it regularly  they didn't write about it regularly  until the u.s government began to leak  it  leaks from the prosecutor's office that  this investigation was going on and then  of course it was banner headlines  but that wasn't until 84 83 or 84. why  can you knowing how the system works how  the government sometimes tries to  influence the media the like why was the  justice department leaking the story at  that point what what did they see as a  as an end to be served  they wanted to  this is my view i mean they would they  would dispute this but i think that they  wanted to create a general feeling of  paranoia among the people who were under  investigation and get them to come  forward and and cooperate  furthermore the story had actually begun  to leak to some of the targets  they had served a subpoena  on one of the lawyers who had been  involved in payoffs to judges  he had been to a defense lawyer and so  they knew it was just a matter of time  until it was going to to leak  and they controlled it very well they  they leaked it to very specific people  and  resulted in massive  international publicity  months and months and months before the  first indictments came down  in the early days before the popular  press got involved in it was this ever a  dangerous story for you to write  oh i don't think so  no i  i don't think that  you know  most of the judges involved here  in this  corruption not all of them but most of  them were really  rather pathetic they had  bad alcohol habits they were alcoholics  what many of them were doing was just  taking enough money to support a good  drinking habit you know 50 100 a day to  pay the bar bill at night  and  no i don't think there was any danger  that  anybody was going to be killed or  anything like that and there were  certainly no threats that came to our  attention in the end out of both the  sting operation and the in the  larger investigation how many  indictments how many convictions there  were 86 or 87 indictments and uh virtual  they have all been convicted with two  exceptions  and among those are 14 judges and the  rest are court personnel and lawyers  when you look at the sentences  what was the attitude by the judicial  system in in policing its own  you got white sentences heavy sentences  oh i think they were extremely heavy i  think that the the sentences were were  very heavy in fact i think the federal  judiciary when it came to dealing with  this problem  was appalled  and many federal judges of course came  out of the state system  and  were they were  genuinely appalled by what they found  yet you indicate that that it continues  the corruption continues  there has unfortunately there's been no  systemic change in the system  it continues to operate pretty much like  it did in the past now this practice of  hustling i mean that's largely gone it  was too apparent and too open  but  whatever the underlying problems were  which i believe stem from the political  selection of judges  the lack of independence of the third  branch of government from the other two  branches  that  the possibility the political fix that  remains it's as prevalent today as it  ever was  we've driven some of the worst crooks i  think off the bench and  and people are a lot more subtle but  this is not the end of judicial  corruption in cook county what's it  going to take for a systemic change  well first i think we'd have to change  the system in which the chief judge is  chosen he's presently elected by the  other judges of the court the ones  he's supposed to be supervising now  that's not really much of a very good  management structure as i'm sure  absolutely every management consultant  in the world would agree  the other is that we need to change the  way in which judges are chosen  we need a it's called a merit selection  system we need to to replace this  political  selection process with a merit selection  system now those are the two biggest  steps that could be taken and of course  neither of those has been taken where do  you see an impetus for change like that  coming from  if this investigation and the massive  publicity  couldn't do it  i don't know what really could  in recent years however it's  been rather  interesting to observe that the  democratic organization has lost  control it's no longer  the monolithic force in chicago that it  once was  in 1984  the democratic  slated candidates for judge  were defeated  by people who happen to have irish names  this is a big irish  city and so if your name is kelly or  o'malley you have a very good shot in a  much better shot than someone with a  non-irish name with a  it just turned out that in 1984 the  organization or the machine slated  people with non-irish names they had  opponents who had  irish names or more irish sounding names  and they won  it's sort of uh  you know left me with the view that  the only  person who could favor the present  system is an anarchist because there are  the organization can't control it  anymore so ultimately when the  organization loses control  and the politicians understand  that they've lost control  perhaps we can have a meaningful reform  but this investigation didn't bring that  about  why did you and james tui decide to turn  this into a book  well we just thought it was  one heck of a story  it's got  the elements of  guys and dolls it's got tragic elements  comic elements  and it's an important story  that had never been told and there was  no other way  one of the reviews said that gee there's  very little new here there's no new  research most of this had been published  before  in various publications including our  own and that's true  but there was no place for  that it was all pulled together and  that's what we  thought we could do in this book is this  a peculiarly chicago story are there  lessons for the country to learn from  this  i would hope  that it's not as bad anywhere else in  the country as it was or is in chicago  but just like police corruption is not  a purely chicago phenomena that  exists everywhere  i assume that this exists  to some extent in every large  metropolitan area  talked a little bit about the  federal  judiciary being outraged by this  do you know for sure or have any sense  that it is  encouraging some review of the process  in other cities around the country  i really have no idea except of course  the reform  there  the bar the organized bar has become  increasingly concerned with ethics  appearances and i think it's probably  having an effect everywhere  but i don't really have any first-hand  knowledge of  what's going on anywhere except in  chicago if you and james tui could write  an epilogue to greylord a few years from  now what would you hope that would be  i would  i would hope that we could find more  heroes  i would hope that we could  that we could find someone  who would  be the hero of the sequel i'd  i'd like to find at least one  good honest person in the system  to make a hero and unfortunately we  didn't have that this time that will  have to wait for the sequel  and uh  in addition to that  ultimately that the system will be  reformed as well or have you given up  hope on that well i would hope  that the system  would be reformed and that that's why we  would have a hero  who finally brought this about  what did you learn by writing the book  anything  yes you always learn things when even if  you before you actually start writing  something it helps you know  clarify the thought process  that's why i see sometimes courts issue  decisions and say that a written opinion  will follow i think that that's wrong  because i think the writing process is  what clarifies  that opinion and the thought process and  you  in the writing process you run against  problems that hadn't occurred to you or  that you hadn't focused on quite as well  and you learn a great deal  and i think  that at one point  it just really dawned on me  how thoroughly  corrupt  this entire system  was i mean we had judges who were  popping off the bench to take payoffs in  the middle of trials  we had in one case a lawyer who was  literally involved and  if you invited him to dinner at your  house he'd case the joint for his  burglar friends he once set up a money  laundering scheme  for uh  some friends of his and he changed uh  some gold cougarands for cash for them  in his office and arranged to have them  hit in the unmugged in the lobby of his  building we had prosecutors who were  authorizing  the bugging of clients by lawyers i mean  to me this is just  just thoroughly a thoroughly distasteful  thing that was exposed here and  it really  having covered it for all these years  writing this book brought that all into  focus  for me  rob warden is co-author with james tui  of greylord justice chicago style thanks  for talking with us thank you  [Music]  this interview with author rob warden  will re-air at 12 25 a.m eastern time  which is 9 25 in the evening for our  viewers on the west coast  stay with us now for coverage of a panel  discussion on
 

